EXT. BARNSTORMING FIELD - CORNFIELD DIAMOND - DAY

1931. A baseball diamond mown into a Nebraska cornfield. A wooden dugout built from a hay wagon. Dust. A crowd of two hundred cheers from the stalks.

SKIPPER GUS LARUE, 60, cap crushed, tobacco in his cheek, leans on the wagon rail. DIZZY PETROV, 25, big-armed pitcher, sits on a bale, an ice pack on his elbow. Little TOBIN, 11, the batboy, hugs a stack of bats three times too large.

SKIPPER
(to the whole dugout)
Gentlemen, we are up by one, and I do not wish to hear anything more about the elbow.

DIZZY
Skipper, it's gone.

SKIPPER
Which elbow?

DIZZY
Mine.

SKIPPER
Dizzy, you pitched a no-hitter in the fourth.

DIZZY
I pitched a no-hitter with a ghost elbow.

TOBIN
Is a ghost elbow bad?

SKIPPER
Tobin, hush.

DIZZY
It's cold, Tobin. And it hurts.

The crowd roars. A ball cracks off a bat. Everyone in the dugout turns to see it sail over the corn.

SKIPPER
(muttering)
Foul. Please, Lord, foul.

TOBIN
It's fair! It's fair!

DIZZY
Tobin, it's a long fly.

SKIPPER
Hush, the two of you. Dizzy, get your glove.

DIZZY
Skipper, I can't.

SKIPPER
Then who?

TOBIN
Me!

SKIPPER
Tobin, you're eleven.

TOBIN
I can throw!

SKIPPER
Tobin, put down the bats.

Tobin drops the bats with a clatter. Dizzy picks up the glove, groaning. The Skipper waves him toward the field.

SKIPPER (CONT'D)
Go. Throw like it's the last one you've got.

DIZZY
It might be.

SKIPPER
Then throw it like you mean it.

Dizzy trudges out. The crowd stands. Tobin climbs the wagon and watches him go, hands in his hair.

TOBIN
He'll do it, right, Skipper?

SKIPPER
He'll do something.
